window.docContents[158].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Relations-to-trigonometric-functions":{"contents":"Source: DLMF §4.28, Relations to Trigonometric Functions.\n\nThe imaginary-argument identities below are complex identities.  In the\ncosecant and secant entries, Mathlib has no dedicated complex reciprocal\nfunction names, so the page uses the transparent local definitions\ncomplexCosecant z = 1/sin z and complexSecant z = 1/cos z.\n\n\\sin(iz)=i\\sinh z.Sine at i z is i times hyperbolic sine.\n\n\\cos(iz)=\\cosh z.Cosine at i z is hyperbolic cosine without an additional factor.\n\n\\tan(iz)=i\\tanh z.The tangent identity follows the same imaginary-argument conversion, away\nfrom poles under the quotient convention.\n\n\\csc(iz)=-i\\operatorname{csch} z.Using the transparent reciprocal definitions, cosecant at i z becomes\n-i times hyperbolic cosecant.\n\n\\sec(iz)=\\operatorname{sech} z.Secant at i z agrees with hyperbolic secant, again using reciprocal\ndefinitions on their pole-free domains.\n\n\\cot(iz)=-i\\coth z.The cotangent identity is the reciprocal counterpart of E10.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity\u0009§4.28 Definitions and Periodicity","header":"Relations to trigonometric functions","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--___4___28-Definitions-and-Periodicity--Relations-to-trigonometric-functions"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--Spherical-triangles":{"contents":"For spherical triangles, lower-case letters denote arc lengths and upper-case\nletters angles.  E8 and E12 are the two cosine laws, E9 is the sine law, and\nE10–E11 are useful rearrangements.  Since every angle and arc is strictly\nbetween 0 and π, all sine denominators are nonzero.  E11 additionally\nexcludes b=π/2 and B=π/2, where Lean's totalized tangent would have a zero\ndenominator; no branch choice or ambiguous inverse-trigonometric solution is\nbeing silently selected.\n\n\\cos a=\\cos b\\cos c+\\sin b\\sin c\\cos A$$\n\n\\frac{\\sin A}{\\sin a}=\\frac{\\sin B}{\\sin b}=\\frac{\\sin C}{\\sin c}$$\n\n\\sin a\\cos B=\\cos b\\sin c-\\sin b\\cos c\\cos A$$\n\n\\cos a\\cos C=\\sin a\\cot b-\\sin C\\cot B$$\n\n\\cos A=-\\cos B\\cos C+\\sin B\\sin C\\cos a$$\n\nThe source figures 4.42.1–4.42.3 illustrate these conventions; they carry no\nadditional numbered identity.  All twelve numbered displays are represented\nabove, with the real-positive specialization recorded explicitly in the\ncoverage manifest.\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.42 Solution of Triangles\u0009§4.42 Solution of Triangles","header":"Spherical triangles","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--___4___42-Solution-of-Triangles--Spherical-triangles"}});